{:sources {"soul-refined.lain" "(load-lain \"model-refined.lain\")\n(load-lain \"lib/behavior-core.lain\")\n(load-lain \"lib/language-context.lain\")\n\n;; One model and one lexical child for the entire open session.\n(define m (model))\n(define model-result (model:output m))\n(define no-user-yet)\n(define raw-user-events)\n(source:initialize raw-user-events :lain.data/empty)\n(session:export :user \"conversation\" :user-message (cell:reference raw-user-events))\n(define user-records (list:vector raw-user-events))\n(define user-ingress\n  (if (> (seq:count user-records) 0)\n      (let [event (seq:last user-records)]\n        (data:record :id (data:get :id event)\n                     :time (- (* 2 (seq:count user-records)) 1)\n                     :role \"user\" :content (data:get :payload event)))\n      no-user-yet))\n(define delivered-events)\n(source:initialize delivered-events :lain.data/empty)\n(define context-cell\n  \"Construct a small live conversation network in Lain. Keep reasoning brief. Return only executable Lain forms, without markdown. Read guide-core-cell and guide-examples-cell for the relational philosophy and operator-examples-cell for executable API examples with expected results. Apply that understanding to this task. Discover the environment bindings below. Your setup must build history with the supplied tested temporal reducers, preserving chronological user messages and acknowledged assistant replies. Connect user-ingress to model requests and define a reusable reply function that delivers to the user, then adds the acknowledged reply to history. Subsequent requests must contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages. Provider thinking and generated source are never conversation replies. Each subsequent model response must be executable Lain calling your reply function with a new assistant message. Conversation requests should include an explicit system message containing the reply-function calling convention you have defined, so future requests know to produce executable source. Do not include setup code or provider thinking in conversation history. Use the same model throughout. The parent binding history retains your generated source code automatically; it is not conversation history. Use a different name for the conversation accumulator. memory-cell is a live compound interface with open writable slots; attach selected strongest values using the concrete memory feedback example in operator-examples-cell. Do not send a request until a user event arrives. Unknown inputs suspend a computation; avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request. model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target. Inspect diagnostics if construction fails.\")\n(define language-introduction-cell\n  \"Lain is a connected dataflow language, not Clojure. Return raw parenthesized forms only; triple-backtick fences are invalid source. A callable is invoked as (f x); (-> x f) writes to the callable cell and does NOT call f. Only binding/argument vectors use literal brackets; construct data vectors with seq:empty and seq:append. Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records or other behavior objects. A scan takes its source as its first argument; do not write events to the scan result. behavior:scan, map, and event-filter produce behavior objects, not ordinary records or vectors. Use behavior:latest-value to project a selected behavior value before passing to ordinary primitives. An optional fallback makes an empty behavior usable without inventing an occurrence. seq:project projects one key, so (seq:project :role xs) returns role strings, not message records. Model messages must be records with string :role and :content. System instructions must be first. The user ingress is a message RECORD, not a behavior or callable. You must capture its occurrences with event:point if you need a temporal source. (define name expression) binds a cell. (define pending) declares an unknown cell. (network [x y] expression) defines a callable. (let [x expression y expression] body) binds locals. (-> value target-cell) connects cells. (if condition yes no) selects a branch. Qualified names avoid editor collisions. Use data:record for immutable plain records, NOT struct, when passing data to primitives. (data:record :key value) constructs a record; (data:get :key record) reads it. seq:empty [] returns an empty vector; seq:append [vector element] appends one element; seq:concat [vector ...] concatenates vectors into one flat vector; for example (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) 1) (seq:append (seq:empty) 2)) returns a vector containing 1 and 2; seq:last [vector] reads the last element. seq:project [key vector] projects a key over a vector. data:equal? [a b] compares values. event:point [id numeric-time value] captures a retained temporal occurrence. Each identity is immutable: later changes at its input do not withdraw earlier occurrences; connect its output to a behavior:source or scan. text:format [template & values] formats %s placeholders; for example (text:format \\\"item %s\\\" 7). Example individual operations: (define values (behavior:source)); (-> (event:point \\\"sample\\\" 1 7) values); (define sums (behavior:scan values + 0)). behavior:source [] creates a reducer cell; connect supported event:point results to it with ->. behavior:scan [source reducer initial] emits a state at EVERY ordered occurrence: each state is the corresponding accumulated prefix, not merely the final state. It uses a pure two-argument Lain callable; maps, filters, and reducers construct values and must not perform IO. Callback invocation uses a frozen temporary network, so callbacks must use their explicit arguments and immutable constants; reading changing external cells does not read the live network. behavior:event-filter [behavior predicate] keeps matching historical occurrences and their values. If a new occurrence is rejected, the last previously accepted occurrence remains available: latest-value of the filtered behavior stays unchanged. The predicate receives each behavior VALUE, which may itself be a vector or record. behavior:map [behavior callable] maps values. behavior:latest-value [behavior optional-fallback] projects its most recent value to an ordinary supported cell; before the first occurrence it returns the optional fallback, or remains unknown if no fallback was supplied. For example (behavior:latest-value values 0). Repeated identical events are suppressed; sources and scans are independent. model:request [record] submits { :id string :messages vector-of-role-content-records } to the existing model. Roles are strings system/user/assistant; content is a string. user-ingress publishes a record with :id, :time (odd integer), :role user, :content. Assistant time should be user time + 1 and assistant id must differ from user id. message-to-user [record] acknowledges a record containing :id :time :role assistant :content and returns that record with :acknowledged true. Its output, not generated source, is the delivered message. source:admit [complete-reply lexical-target support-cell-reference] admits complete forms sequentially, preserving earlier forms if a later one fails. environment:overview [environment] lists visible binding titles, headlines and references, including unknowns. cell:reference [cell] yields an inspectable reference without waiting for its value. inspect:cell [reference] exposes only :edge, :value (strongest value), :inputs (producing connections), and :outputs (consuming connections). cell:value [reference] connects that edge's strongest value directly to an ordinary cell. data:snapshot [compound] resolves a finite compound into a plain record; unknown slots remain pending. Use inspect:cell for edge/value/input/output inspection.\")\n(define model:request\n  (network [request] (io-model:input ((slot :worker) m) request)))\n(define message-to-user\n  (network [message]\n    (let [receipt (data:snapshot (message:deliver message (cell:reference delivered-events)))]\n      (if (data:equal? (data:get :status receipt) :delivered)\n          (data:merge message (data:record :acknowledged true))\n          no-user-yet))))\n(define observe-cell)\n(define construction-review-cell\n  \"A previous generated setup compiled but did not connect requests or history. Diagnostic review: each call to behavior:source creates a DISTINCT source, so writing to another unnamed instance does not feed a previously created scan. Event identity and time must vary with actual events; constants user/1 and assistant/2 cannot identify three turns. A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before feeding a temporal source. A callable inside behavior:map or scan is evaluated as a pure transformation; model:request and message-to-user must run in the live network, never inside a temporal transformation callback. seq:project with key :role returns strings and therefore is unsuitable as the request messages vector. No generated definitions from previous attempts exist in this fresh child.\")\n(define reducer-verification-cell\n  \"Verified through compiled Lain, not a simulated conversation: a numeric source with occurrences 1@1 and 2@2, scanned with seq:append and an empty vector seed, emits prefix values [1] and [1,2]. Filtering those prefix values by whether their last number is greater than 1 keeps [1,2]. A later 0@3 is rejected by that predicate, leaving the filtered latest value [1,2]. The unfiltered scan advances to [1,2,0]. Exact replay produces no new values, and another source remains independent. Each call to behavior:source creates a new independent instance. A scan compiled into the lexical admission target also reduced an occurrence 5@1 with + and seed 0 to 5.\")\n(define experiment-diagnostic-cell)\n(source:initialize experiment-diagnostic-cell :not-started)\n;; The parent history is generated source, distinct from conversation messages.\n(define written-code-events (behavior:source))\n(define written-code-prefixes (behavior:scan written-code-events seq:append (seq:empty)))\n(define code-history-cell (behavior:latest-value written-code-prefixes (seq:empty)))\n(-> (event:point (data:get :response-sequence model-result)\n                 (data:get :response-sequence model-result)\n                 (data:record :sequence (data:get :response-sequence model-result)\n                              :request-id (data:get :request-id model-result)\n                              :source (model:action m)\n                              :kind (data:get :kind model-result)))\n    written-code-events)\n(define memory-cell\n  (struct :user (cell:reference user-ingress)\n               :model-output (cell:reference (model:output m))\n               :deliveries (cell:reference delivered-events)\n               :code-history (cell:reference code-history-cell)\n               :diagnostics (cell:reference observe-cell)))\n(define construct-concept-of\n  (network [name content]\n    (network [environment] (extend-env environment (env:slot name content)))))\n(define default-environment\n  (|> (empty-env)\n      (construct-concept-of \"context-cell\" context-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"guide-core-cell\" guide-core-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"guide-examples-cell\" guide-examples-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"operator-examples-cell\" operator-examples-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"construction-review-cell\" construction-review-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"reducer-verification-cell\" reducer-verification-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"language-introduction-cell\" language-introduction-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"observe-cell\" observe-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"experiment-diagnostic-cell\" experiment-diagnostic-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"memory-cell\" memory-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"history\" code-history-cell)\n      (construct-concept-of \"cell:value\" cell:value)\n      (construct-concept-of \"cell:reference\" cell:reference)\n      (construct-concept-of \"data:snapshot\" data:snapshot)\n      (construct-concept-of \"user-ingress\" user-ingress)\n      (construct-concept-of \"model:request\" model:request)\n      (construct-concept-of \"message-to-user\" message-to-user)\n      (construct-concept-of \"behavior:source\" behavior:source)\n      (construct-concept-of \"behavior:scan\" behavior:scan)\n      (construct-concept-of \"behavior:map\" behavior:map)\n      (construct-concept-of \"behavior:event-filter\" behavior:event-filter)\n      (construct-concept-of \"behavior:latest-value\" behavior:latest-value)\n      (construct-concept-of \"event:point\" event:point)\n      (construct-concept-of \"text:format\" text:format)\n      (construct-concept-of \"data:record\" data:record)\n      (construct-concept-of \"data:get\" data:get)\n      (construct-concept-of \"data:equal?\" data:equal?)\n      (construct-concept-of \"seq:empty\" seq:empty)\n      (construct-concept-of \"seq:append\" seq:append)\n      (construct-concept-of \"seq:concat\" seq:concat)\n      (construct-concept-of \"seq:last\" seq:last)\n      (construct-concept-of \"seq:project\" seq:project)\n      (construct-concept-of \"source:admit\" source:admit)\n      (construct-concept-of \"environment:overview\" environment:overview)\n      (construct-concept-of \"inspect:cell\" inspect:cell)))\n(define generated-environment (lexical-child default-environment))\n;; Bootstrap request contains exactly the environment description.\n(model:request (data:record :id \"setup\" :text (cell:first (environment:overview default-environment))))\n(-> (source:admit (model:output m) generated-environment\n                  (cell:reference (model:output m))) observe-cell)\n", "model-refined.lain" "(load-lain \"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\")\n(load-lain \"lib/model-io.lain\")\n(load-lain \"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/tracers.lain\")\n\n(define model\n  (network []\n    (let-cell [input output worker]\n      (-> (io-model:worker) worker)\n      (io-model:input worker input)\n      (io-model:output worker output)\n      (struct :worker worker :input input\n              :output output\n              :thinking (prim-slot :thinking output)\n              :actions (prim-slot :text output)))))\n\n(define model:input (slot :input))\n(define model:output (slot :output))\n(define model:thinking (slot :thinking))\n(define model:action (slot :actions))\n", "lib/behavior-core.lain" ";; Namespaced operators for compositions that already export history.\n(load-primitive-environment \"src/lain_harness/behavior.clj\" :lain-harness.behavior/primitive-bindings 1)\n", "lib/language-context.lain" ";; Generated by tools/build_language_context.py from the recorded guide/catalog sources.\n(define guide-core-cell\n  \"These are language documentation excerpts, not source to admit verbatim. The early guide's schematic helpers are not additional capabilities. For temporal reduction use the qualified behavior:* APIs documented in operator-examples-cell; the guide's bounded visualization history is a separate operator. The combinator library is already loaded. Return executable Lain only, without Markdown fences.\\n\\nSource: docs/how-to-think-in-lain.md; SHA256 af917bbd5450ab475b5179ad5be60f1ba2c5e72966f0b3d00d9686eeabd9eb94.\\n## 1. Core Philosophy: Compose Relationships into Expressions\\nLain is a Lisp-like, propagator-based coordination language for describing high-level interactions between components.\\nIts central programming abstraction is the composition of transformations and relationships. Drawing on lambda calculus and functional composition, Lain allows programmers to construct, connect, and transform propagator networks through reusable abstractions.\\nCompose verbs into sentences. Do not concatenate nouns into names.\\nA Lain program should read like a meaningful expression describing how components relate, constrain, coordinate, and transform one another.\\nThink of a synthesizer, an electrical circuit, or a chemical reaction. The essential structure emerges from how components interact and what relationships their interactions maintain.\\nCells provide the medium through which information propagates. They support the network’s operation, while the program’s primary expression concerns the relationships between its components.\\nThis philosophy is related to Alan Kay’s emphasis on messaging and interaction in object-oriented programming. Lain extends this perspective through compositional network transformations and multidirectional information propagation.\\nFundamental principles:\\n* Think in transformations, connections, constraints, and coordination.\\n* Express complex behavior by composing smaller relationships.\\n* Treat network topology as part of the program’s meaning.\\n* Preserve the distinction between a relationship and a particular procedure for evaluating it.\\n* Use cells and internal machinery when their semantics require them.\\n* Prefer reusable relational abstractions over specialized containers.\\nThe ideal Lain program communicates what relationships hold, how they compose, and what information can propagate through them.\\n\\n⸻\\n\\n\\n## 3. Think Like a Circuit Designer\\nConceive a Lain program as a network of interacting transformations.\\nConsider a schematic chemical relationship:\\n2 H₂O → H₂ + 2 OH\\nThis is an illustrative transformation expression, not a chemically balanced reaction.\\nA conceptual Lain representation might be:\\n```clojure\\n;; Schematic: the molecular operations require application definitions.\\n(network [water hydrogen hydroxide]\\n  (<-> (h2-of water) hydrogen)\\n  (<-> (molecule:* 2 (oh-of water)) hydroxide))\\n```\\nThis example is schematic and does not establish the chemical or operational semantics of the operators.\\nIts purpose is to demonstrate that the network exposes relationships between participants.\\nThe same design perspective applies to electrical circuits, reactive interfaces, constraint systems, and distributed coordination.\\nBefore implementation, identify:\\n1. What relationships must hold?\\n2. Which transformations express those relationships?\\n3. Which components participate?\\n4. How can the transformations be composed?\\n5. Which relationships have useful symmetries?\\n6. What information can be inferred from different endpoints?\\nDefer decisions about intermediate cells, storage, and internal plumbing until the relational structure is clear.\\nHeuristic: Begin by describing the network’s meaning. Derive its implementation structure from that description.\\n\\n⸻\\n\\n\\n## 4. Declare Relationships, Not Execution Sequences\\nLain networks describe concurrently existing relationships within activated topology. An unselected conditional body need not be expanded.\\nThe textual order of network declarations should not be interpreted as an imperative execution sequence. Lexical scope, name binding, library loading, and explicit effect boundaries still matter; dataflow does not erase those constraints.\\nConsider:\\n```clojure\\n;; Schematic: f, g, and h must be defined by the application.\\n(define e\\n  (network [a b c]\\n    (-> (f a) b)\\n    (-> (g b) c)\\n    (-> (h c) a)\\n    (list a b c)))\\n```\\nThese declarations describe network topology.\\nThey do not imply that the first relationship must finish before the second begins.\\nInformation propagates according to the dependencies and propagation semantics of the network.\\nReason about topology\\nWhen reading or writing a network:\\n* Treat declarations as simultaneously existing relationships.\\n* Represent dependencies through connections.\\n* Allow propagation to follow available information.\\n* Avoid using source-code order to express control flow.\\n* Make ordering, buffering, and timing explicit when necessary.\\n* Consider feedback, convergence, and conflicting information.\\nA network is a persistent organization of relationships whose information state may evolve.\\nAnti-pattern: imperative thinking disguised as propagators\\nAn agent may translate each step of a procedural algorithm into a separate propagator while retaining the original execution sequence.\\nThis can produce syntactically valid Lain code with little benefit from the language’s relational capabilities.\\nCorrection: Reconsider the problem as a network of constraints and interactions. Determine which dependencies are intrinsic to the domain and which were introduced by the procedural decomposition.\\n\\n⸻\\n\\n\\n## 8. Introduce Cells for Meaningful State, Not Procedural Plumbing\\nCells are fundamental to information propagation.\\nHowever, an agent should not begin implementation by allocating a large collection of cells and subsequently inventing a protocol to contain them.\\nThis is a common failure mode.\\nCell-first design\\nA problematic approach:\\n1. Create many intermediate cells.\\n2. Assign a procedural stage to each cell.\\n3. Connect the cells into a fixed pipeline.\\n4. Wrap the entire structure in a large named protocol.\\nThis reproduces a conventional data-processing architecture using propagator machinery.\\nRelationship-first design\\nA preferable approach:\\n1. Identify the domain relationships.\\n2. Find existing transformations that express them.\\n3. Compose those transformations.\\n4. Introduce cells where the network requires independently meaningful information state.\\n5. Encapsulate internal plumbing behind reusable abstractions.\\nA dedicated cell is appropriate when it represents:\\n* Independently retained information\\n* Shared knowledge among multiple propagators\\n* A meaningful network endpoint\\n* Feedback state\\n* Explicit temporal state\\n* Information that must be accumulated or refined\\nHeuristic: Every explicit cell should have a clear semantic role.\\nIf a cell exists only to carry a temporary value between two composable transformations, consider eliminating it from the high-level expression.\\n\\n⸻\\n\\n\\n## 9. Make Time, History, and State Explicit\\nConcurrent relationships require explicit treatment of temporal behavior.\\nA propagator network may evolve as information becomes available, changes, or is refined.\\nTemporal operations should be expressed as transformations with clear semantics.\\nFor example:\\n(-> (|> op-a\\n        slot-accessor\\n        (history 5))\\n    slot-full-history)\\n`|>` and `(history n)` are implemented by combinator.lain. Here `(history 5)` means at most five incoming publications per application, not a five-second time window. The domain-specific source/accessor names remain schematic.\\nPotential temporal transformations include:\\n* Sampling and debouncing\\n* Delaying and buffering\\n* Accumulating and windowing\\n* Deduplicating and batching\\n* Synchronizing and retaining history\\nThese transformations can become reusable coordination operators.\\nHeuristics:\\n* Use explicit stateful transformations when information must persist.\\n* Define event ordering and retention behavior.\\n* Avoid relying on incidental scheduling order.\\n* Represent meaningful temporal state through appropriate cells or network abstractions.\\n* Consider unbounded history and memory growth.\\n* Distinguish information refinement from temporal replacement where relevant.\\nWhenever timing affects correctness, make it part of the declared network semantics.\\n\\n⸻\\n\\n\\n## 11. Preserve Abstraction Boundaries\\nA high-level Lain program should expose the intention of a network while hiding incidental machinery.\\nAvoid manipulating internal data representations when suitable constructors, accessors, or network transformations exist.\\nPrefer:\\n(-> (output-of component-a)\\n    (input-of component-b))\\nover manually extracting storage, allocating intermediate structures, and installing connections.\\nData abstraction rules\\n* Construct structured values through explicit constructors.\\n* Access structured values through explicit accessors.\\n* Never directly construct internal data structures in the middle of unrelated network logic.\\n* Keep representation details behind public abstractions.\\n* Encapsulate internal network topology when callers do not need to know it.\\n* Use reusable combinators for recurring coordination patterns.\\nA high-level network should remain understandable even if the internal representation of its cells or propagators changes.\\nHeuristic: Abstractions should preserve the meaning of relationships while allowing their implementation to evolve.\\n\\n⸻\\n\\n\\n## 13. Practical Reasoning Procedure for AI Agents\\nWhen implementing a feature in Lain, follow this reasoning process.\\nStep 1 — Express the problem as relationships\\nDescribe what the system must maintain, relate, constrain, communicate, or transform.\\nBegin with a compact conceptual network.\\nDo not start by designing data structures or protocols.\\nStep 2 — Identify the verbs\\nFind the smallest meaningful transformations and coordination operations.\\nSearch for existing functions, propagators, and network combinators that express them.\\nStep 3 — Compose a sentence\\nTry to express the intended behavior through the composition of those operations.\\nAsk:\\nCan the implementation read as a meaningful relational expression?\\nPrefer a short, compositional network expression over a large named protocol.\\nStep 4 — Search for symmetry\\nDetermine whether relationships support inference in multiple directions.\\nIdentify the conditions under which each direction is valid.\\nPreserve multidirectionality when it is semantically meaningful.\\nStep 5 — Construct the network\\nUse network, ->, <->, and appropriate existing combinators to express the coordination.\\nTreat declarations as concurrently existing relationships.\\nUse only operators supported by the actual implementation.\\nStep 6 — Introduce necessary state\\nAdd cells for meaningful information state, shared knowledge, feedback, or retention.\\nAvoid intermediate cells that merely reproduce a procedural pipeline.\\nStep 7 — Verify propagation semantics\\nCheck for:\\n* Accidental dependencies on evaluation order\\n* Cyclic propagation\\n* Convergence assumptions\\n* Conflicting information\\n* Unsupported inverse operations\\n* Unbounded state or history\\n* Ambiguous timing behavior\\nDo not assume scheduling, conflict-resolution, or truth-maintenance mechanisms that have not been implemented.\\nStep 8 — Add supporting capabilities\\nCompose tracing, observability, validation, and other crosscutting behavior where appropriate.\\nKeep the primary domain relationships visible.\\nStep 9 — Simplify the expression\\nLook for redundant plumbing, unnecessary cells, repeated coordination patterns, and overly specialized protocols.\\nAsk whether existing transformations can be composed more directly.\\nStep 10 — Review the conceptual model\\nBefore finalizing, ask:\\nAm I describing a network of relationships, or have I merely translated an imperative algorithm into propagator syntax?\\nIf the latter, reconsider the decomposition.\\n\\n⸻\\n\\n\\n## 16. The Guiding Principle\\nLain is a language for composing transformations into networks of coordinated relationships.\\nIts programming style should encourage thinking in terms of circuits, constraints, synthesis, and information propagation.\\nCompose verbs into sentences, rather than concatenate nouns into names.\\nA program should express how relationships are established, maintained, constrained, and transformed.\\nThe quality of a Lain program should be judged by how directly its structure communicates those relationships.\\nPrefer meaningful transformations, justified symmetry, generic coordination operators, and declarative composition.\\nUse cells, protocols, storage, and lower-level machinery in service of the network’s semantics.\\nThe ideal Lain program is not a taxonomy of computational objects or a disguised sequence of commands. It is a compositional expression of the relationships that constitute a system.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n## 17. Write Against the Current Language\\n\\nThe following examples use the production Compiler-2 source/session loader. Run each complete example in a fresh session from the repository root; follow-up snippets explicitly extend that same session. Inspect the named result bindings after propagation. At the raw compiler boundary, `define` produces a binding receipt, so a final `answer` expression makes the intended value explicit.\\n\\n### Core syntax and optional libraries\\n\\n| Intention | Current form | Important distinction |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Declare a reusable network | `(define f (network [x] (+ x 1)))` | A name belongs in `define`, not between `network` and its parameters. |\\n| Return a result | The final body expression | There is no separate output vector after the parameters. Return `(list ...)` when those cell connections form the intended interface. |\\n| Introduce unknown endpoints | `(define input)`, `(def-cells a b)`, `(let-cell [local] ...)` | An unresolved cell is information not yet available, not an empty list or an assignment variable. |\\n| Select a result | `(if condition then-expression else-expression)` | Exactly three operands; use a boolean-valued predicate. |\\n| Select among ordered cases | `(cond [test expression test expression else expression])` | One vector of pairs; the default is bare `else`, not Clojure's `:else`. |\\n| Install guarded topology | `(when guard body ...)` | It waits for usable information, not specifically boolean true. |\\n| Apply a callable to cells | `(apply f argument-list)` | Exactly one linked argument list; the cells remain connected. |\\n| Declare rest parameters | `(network [head & tail] ...)` | The rest parameter is a linked list, not a host vector. |\\n| Construct a complete list | `(list)`, `(list 1 2)` | The constructor supplies a known empty terminal tail. |\\n| Construct an open prefix | `(cons head unresolved-tail)` | Unknown tail information must not be interpreted as a final length. |\\n| Ask whether a structure is finished | `(empty-list? tail)` for lists; no form for structs | A struct discovers its slots through use, so completeness needs an explicit cell (§19). |\\n\\nLain uses an EDN-backed reader; do not assume every Clojure special form exists. In particular, the removed `def`, `def-net`, `def-constraint`, `cell-expr`, `compound`, and `do` forms are not substitutes for current syntax. Use keywords for the slot keys shown here rather than assuming a host quote form.\\n\\n`|>`, `compose`, `struct`, `slot`, `<->`, unary predicate `switch`, bounded `history`, and `constraint:+` belong to the compositional library/extension:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n(define increment (network [x] (+ x 1)))\\n(define double (network [x] (* x 2)))\\n(define answer (|> 3 increment double))\\nanswer\\n;; 8: pipe stages apply left to right.\\n```\\n\\n`((compose increment double) 3)` applies the rightmost stage first and produces `7`. Names such as `normalize`, `observe`, and `sender-slot-of` in the earlier domain illustrations still need application definitions.\\n\\n\")\n\n(define guide-examples-cell\n  \"These are language documentation excerpts, not source to admit verbatim. The early guide's schematic helpers are not additional capabilities. For temporal reduction use the qualified behavior:* APIs documented in operator-examples-cell; the guide's bounded visualization history is a separate operator. The combinator library is already loaded. Return executable Lain only, without Markdown fences.\\n\\nSource: docs/how-to-think-in-lain-examples.md; SHA256 7d414d58948c1bda79f5f32140625ce4399e8fc195e369de8c9f0d2d0c23c57d.\\n## 1. Core philosophy: compose relationships, not computations\\n\\nThe useful object is a reusable transformation between endpoints, not a call\\nsequence. Two independent networks compose into a third.\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(define celsius->fahrenheit (network [c] (+ (* 1.8 c) 32)))\\n(define wind-chill-adjust (network [f] (- f 2)))\\n(define room-temp 20)\\n(define answer (|> room-temp celsius->fahrenheit wind-chill-adjust))\\n;; => answer 66.0\\n```\\n\\n\\n## 2. The three semantic layers: verbs, relations, sentences\\n\\n* verbs — factories that build transformations (`network`, `struct`, `slot`, `|>`, `compose`);\\n* relations — named endpoints that hold information (`def-cells`, `let-cell`, `<->`);\\n* sentences — statements that connect them (`->`, applications, assertions).\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(define scale (network [x] (* x 3)))\\n(define shift (network [x] (+ x 10)))\\n(def-cells raw result)\\n(-> 4 raw)\\n(-> (|> raw scale shift) result)\\n;; => result 22\\n```\\n\\n\\n## 4. Declare relationships, not sequences\\n\\nThe same three sentences in a different textual order produce the same network\\nand the same answer. Textual order never schedules execution.\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(def-cells a b c)\\n(-> 3 a)\\n(-> (+ a 1) b)\\n(-> (* b 2) c)\\n;; => c 8\\n```\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(def-cells a b c)\\n(-> (* b 2) c)\\n(-> 3 a)\\n(-> (+ a 1) b)\\n;; => c 8\\n```\\n\\n\\n## 7. Network transformation is compositional abstraction\\n\\n`|>` is left-to-right; `compose` returns a stage and applies right-to-left.\\nBoth take time to learn — that is normal, not a sign of a broken abstraction.\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(define increment (network [x] (+ x 1)))\\n(define double (network [x] (* x 2)))\\n(define piped (|> 3 increment double))\\n(define composed ((compose increment double) 3))\\n;; => piped 8\\n;; => composed 7\\n```\\n\\n\\n## 8. Cells are for meaningful state, not plumbing\\n\\nPlumb a transient value with `let-cell` inside one relationship. Reach for a\\nretained cell when the value is part of the public interface or must accumulate\\ninformation.\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(define average\\n  (network [a b]\\n    (let-cell [sum]\\n      (constraint:+ a b sum)\\n      (/ sum 2))))\\n(define answer (average 3 7))\\n;; => answer 5\\n```\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(def-cells a b)\\n(-> 1 a)\\n(-> a b)\\n(define answer b)\\n;; => answer 1\\n```\\n\\n\\n## 11. Preserve abstraction boundaries\\n\\nA slot is a connected view of one field. The owner stays an opaque compound;\\nnothing reaches into its internals.\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(define incoming)\\n(define object (struct :nested (struct :value incoming)))\\n(-> 42 (|> object (slot :nested) (slot :value)))\\n;; => incoming 42\\n```\\n\\n\\n## 12. Build a vocabulary of small transformations\\n\\nSmall named networks become the project's vocabulary and compose without\\nrewriting the surrounding structure.\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(define increment (network [x] (+ x 1)))\\n(define square (network [x] (* x x)))\\n(define negate (network [x] (- x)))\\n(define answer (|> 5 increment square negate))\\n;; => answer -36\\n```\\n\\n\\n## 13. Practical reasoning procedure\\n\\nExpose endpoints, declare the relationship, compose existing stages, then verify\\nwith an assertion.\\n\\n```lain\\n(load-lain \\\"propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain\\\")\\n\\n(def-cells width height perimeter)\\n(define perimeter-of (network [w h] (* 2 (+ w h))))\\n(-> 3 width)\\n(-> 4 height)\\n(-> (perimeter-of width height) perimeter)\\n;; => perimeter 14\\n```\\n\\n\")\n\n(define operator-examples-cell\n  \"Concrete executable examples and expected results for the advertised operators. Each example is independent and uses doc:* names. Core libraries and experiment capabilities are already installed. Tests compile these exact sources; no example implements a conversation history network. Effects are tested with controlled transport and the real session delivery extension. Read expected observations as test metadata, not additional Lain forms.\\n\\nOperators: define, network, let, if\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:increment (network [x] (let [next (+ x 1)] (if (> next 0) next 0))))\\n(define doc:incremented (doc:increment 4))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:incremented\\\" :kind :value :expected 5}]\\n\\nOperators: ->, def-cells\\nLain source:\\n(def-cells doc:raw doc:forwarded)\\n(-> 7 doc:raw)\\n(-> doc:raw doc:forwarded)\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:forwarded\\\" :kind :value :expected 7}]\\n\\nOperators: |>, compose\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:plus-one (network [x] (+ x 1)))\\n(define doc:twice (network [x] (* x 2)))\\n(define doc:piped (|> 3 doc:plus-one doc:twice))\\n(define doc:composed ((compose doc:plus-one doc:twice) 3))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:piped\\\" :kind :value :expected 8} {:binding \\\"doc:composed\\\" :kind :value :expected 7}]\\n\\nOperators: struct, slot\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:port)\\n(define doc:object (struct :nested (struct :value doc:port)))\\n(-> 42 (|> doc:object (slot :nested) (slot :value)))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:port\\\" :kind :value :expected 42}]\\n\\nOperators: <->\\nLain source:\\n(def-cells doc:left doc:right)\\n(<-> doc:left doc:right)\\n(-> 9 doc:right)\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:left\\\" :kind :value :expected 9}]\\n\\nOperators: let-cell, constraint:+\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:average (network [a b] (let-cell [total] (constraint:+ a b total) (/ total 2))))\\n(define doc:average-value (doc:average 3 7))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:average-value\\\" :kind :value :expected 5}]\\n\\nOperators: cond\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:classification (cond [(< 0 0) :negative (= 0 0) :zero else :positive]))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:classification\\\" :kind :value :expected :zero}]\\n\\nOperators: list, car, cdr\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:list-first (car (list 4 5)))\\n(define doc:list-second (car (cdr (list 4 5))))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:list-first\\\" :kind :value :expected 4} {:binding \\\"doc:list-second\\\" :kind :value :expected 5}]\\n\\nOperators: +, -, *, /, >, <, =\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:arithmetic (+ (* 2 3) (- 8 5) (/ 4 2)))\\n(define doc:comparison (if (> 3 2) (< 1 2) (= 0 1)))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:arithmetic\\\" :kind :value :expected 11} {:binding \\\"doc:comparison\\\" :kind :value :expected true}]\\n\\nOperators: data:record\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:record (data:record :score 7 :label \\\"north\\\"))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:record\\\" :kind :value :expected {:score 7 :label \\\"north\\\"}}]\\n\\nOperators: data:get\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:field (data:get :score (data:record :score 7)))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:field\\\" :kind :value :expected 7}]\\n\\nOperators: data:equal?\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:equal (data:equal? (data:record :score 7) (data:record :score 7)))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:binding \\\"doc:equal\\\" :kind :value :expected true}]\\n\\nOperators: data:merge\\nLain source:\\n(define doc:merged (data:merge (data:record :a 1) (data:record :b 2)))\\nExpected observations (test metadata):\\n[{:bindin